    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d310">

                <pb facs="0506" n="472" xml:id="pg472"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Germany)/11–1148:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Douglas</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">us urgent</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1948-11-11T14:00:00+00:00">November 11,
                            1948—2 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4824. 1. On arrival in London yesterday afternoon<anchor n="1" xml:id="d310-f1"/>
                    I was met with “stink bomb” in announcement Instrument of Trusteeship.<anchor
                        n="2" xml:id="d310-f2"/> In view of strong French protest (Paris 5823,
                    November 10, repeated London 1227<anchor n="3" xml:id="d310-f3"/>) I believe
                    they will undoubtedly seek to place subject on agenda Ruhr discussions which
                    open this afternoon and may demand its modification as <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >sine qua non</hi> to their participation. Moreover even though they do not
                    take any extreme position, at first blush I don’t see how conference can duck
                    discussing the decision stated in the preamble of the trusteeship instrument. In
                    view of this, I feel that matter should have urgent consideration in Department
                    in order that there may be speedy decision and definite instructions sent to us
                    here.</p>
                <p>2. I understand the trusteeship arrangement is to become effective
                    immediately.</p>
                <p>3. You know all the nuances of the situation.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Douglas</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d310-f1" n="1" xml:id="d310-f1-content">
                <p>Ambassador Douglas had been in Washington briefly on consultation.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d310-f2" n="2" xml:id="d310-f2-content">
                <p>The reference here is presumably to the announcement of Military Government Law
                    No. 75 on the reorganization of the German coal, iron and steel industries; see
                    the editorial note, <ref target="#pg465">p. 465</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d310-f3" n="3" xml:id="d310-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed. It reported that Foreign Minister Schuman had urgently called
                    Ambassador Caffery to his office that evening to hand him a communication
                    energetically protesting the action of American and British occupation
                    authorities in issuing U.S.–U.K. Military Government Law No. 75
                    (862.60/11–1048). In his telegram 5836, November 11, from Paris, not printed,
                    Caffery reported that in handing the communication to him, Schuman had observed
                    that the three governments appeared to have been cooperating in the matter and
                    expressed incredulity that the Americans and British would face him with a <hi
                        rendition="#italic">fait accompli</hi> just two days before the convening of
                    the London Conference on the Ruhr. Schuman warned that the Communists and
                    Gaullists would surely exploit the announcement. (740.00119 Control
                    (Germany)/11–1148)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
